.TH TOASTSAVER 1 "November 2026" "toastsaver 1.12.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
toastsaver \- retro screensavers for Wayland desktops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B toastsaver
.RI [ SAVER ]
.br
.B toastsaver-picker
.br
.B toastsaver-settings
.br
.B toastsaver-idle
.RB [ \-\-now
.RI [ SAVER ]
|
.B \-\-check
|
.B \-\-restart
|
.BR \-\-stop ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B toastsaver
is a collection of screensavers: flying toasters, a rainbow waffle cat, 3D pipes,
a cozy bookshelf, a dice tray, an aquarium, birds in a summer sky, a starfield, mystify,
matrix rain, a lava lamp, a volcano, germs under a microscope and a bouncing name banner.
All artwork is drawn by the program.
.PP
At login,
.B toastsaver-idle
is started from
.IR /etc/xdg/autostart .
It runs
.BR swayidle (1),
starts the chosen saver after a period without input, stops it on any input,
and later turns the display off with
.BR wlopm (1)
if it is installed.
.PP
.B toastsaver-picker
opens the app: a fullscreen picker with big tiles, designed for children.
Tapping a picture plays that saver full screen without changing anything.
Each tile's heart keeps that saver in the screensaver: with one heart, SAVER is
that saver; with several, SAVER is
.B random
and RANDOM_SAVERS lists the ones that take turns.
The gear in its top-left corner opens the settings page (on/off, timings, the clock
and power saving). A saver with settings of its own, such as the books' reading speed
or the germs' cells, bubbles and splash, has a sliders button on its tile (or press O
on the highlighted tile) that opens its own page.
.B toastsaver-settings
does the same as
.BR toastsaver-picker .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-windowed
Use a window instead of fullscreen. Keys and clicks no longer quit it: Esc or Q does.
For a saver with settings of its own,
.B O
opens a panel of them while it runs: Up and Down pick one, Left and Right change it,
Enter saves them to the settings file, and Backspace puts the defaults back. Settings
shown in yellow are read when the saver starts, so changing one starts it over. While the panel is
open, a saver that can (germs) draws over itself what the picked setting does: how far everyone
sees, how far bubbles fly, and so on.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List saver names.
.TP
.BI \-\-set " KEY=VALUE"
Change a setting and save it.
.TP
.B \-\-desktop\-icon
Put a Screensavers icon on the desktop.
.TP
.BI \-\-snapshot " FILE.png"
Render a frame to a PNG file without a display (with
.BI \-\-size " WxH"
and
.BI \-\-seconds " N"
).
.TP
.BI \-\-bench " [SAVER|all] [FRAMES]"
Diagnostic: print average step and draw time per frame for each saver (1920x1080 unless
.BI \-\-size
is given), on a hidden window.
.SH SAVERS
toasters, rainbow, pipes, books, dice, aquarium, birds, starfield, mystify, matrix, lava,
volcano, city, germs, name, and random.
.B random
picks a different saver each time, only from those listed in RANDOM_SAVERS
(every saver when it is empty), and not the same one twice in a row. While the
screen stays idle it moves on to another every CYCLE_MINUTES.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/toastsaver.conf
Settings, in shell syntax: ENABLED, SAVER, NAME, IDLE_MINUTES,
SCREEN_OFF_MINUTES, CLOCK, CLOCK_24H, FPS (a frame-rate cap, 30 by default; 0 means as fast as the
display, 30 saves power on a Pi) and RANDOM_SAVERS (a comma-separated
list of savers for SAVER=random; empty means all, unknown names are ignored) and
CYCLE_MINUTES (how often SAVER=random switches savers, 10 by default; 0 means never).
Minutes settings go up to 9999.
.IP
Each saver's own settings start with its name. Books: BOOK_WORDS_PER_MINUTE (reading speed).
Germs: GERM_CELLS, GERM_HELPERS and GERM_SEEDS (how many of each at the start), GERM_MAX (the
most at once; cells divide up to it), GERM_SPEED and GERM_HELPER_SPEED (how fast germs and
helpers move), GERM_SIGHT (how far everyone notices each other), GERM_RELOAD (how often a helper
blows a bubble), GERM_RANGE, GERM_POP (1 pops germs with a splash; 0 washes the one germ a bubble
hits back into a cell, as the saver first did), GERM_SPLASH (how far a bursting bubble reaches; 0 is only the germ
it hits), GERM_TRAIL (how long the germs' trails last for helpers to follow; 0 is none),
GERM_GROWTH (how fast cells divide to refill the slide) and GERM_WRAP. A value out of range is pulled into range.
Created on first change.
.SH NOTES
Needs a Wayland compositor that supports idle notification, such as labwc,
Wayfire, sway or Hyprland. It is not a lock screen.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR swayidle (1),
.BR wlopm (1)
.PP
https://github.com/piyh/toastsaver
